{"from": "text:mishnah-sukkah-5-2-great-lamps", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-08-12-great-lamps", "grade": "B", "note": "Sukkah 5:2-3: golden candelabra in the Women's Courtyard lit with wicks from priests' worn trousers, 'so bright that there was not a courtyard in Jerusalem that was not illuminated from the light of the Place of the Drawing of the Water' - the great-lamp rite of the feast against which 'I am the light of the world' is spoken. It attests the rite, never the saying. Compiled c. 200 CE and may not describe 30s CE practice exactly."}
{"from": "text:mishnah-shekalim-6-1", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-08-20-taught-in-treasury", "grade": "B", "note": "Shekalim 6:1: 'There were thirteen collection horns, narrow at the top and wide at the bottom, into which were placed the shekels' - the treasury (gazophylakion) as a defined area of trumpet-shaped chests in the Temple, where teaching could take place. Reused node; compiled c. 200 CE and may not describe 30s CE practice exactly."}
{"from": "site:second-temple", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-08-20-taught-in-treasury", "grade": "B", "note": "The Second Temple stood in Jerusalem through the 30s CE (reused node); it attests the setting of the treasury teaching."}
{"from": "site:second-temple", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-08-59-stoning-attempt", "grade": "B", "note": "The Second Temple stood in Jerusalem through the 30s CE (reused node); it attests the setting of the confrontation, not the stoning attempt itself."}
{"from": "text:mishnah-sanhedrin-7-5", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-08-59-stoning-attempt", "grade": "C", "note": "Sanhedrin 7:5 places blasphemy among capital cases ('One who blasphemes... is not liable unless he utters the name of God and curses it'), with stoning as the court's execution - the custom behind the crowd's reaction to 'before Abraham was, I AM'. It attests the custom, not that the stones were picked up. Compiled c. 200 CE and may not describe the practice of the 30s CE exactly."}
{"from": "text:mishnah-sanhedrin-7-5", "type": "complicates", "to": "claim:jn-08-59-stoning-attempt", "note": "Tension: Sanhedrin 7:5 holds a blasphemer liable only when he 'utters the name of God and curses it' - the explicit divine Name. John 8:58's 'I AM' (ego eimi) does not obviously pronounce the Name, so whether the saying meets the mishnah's definition is unresolved. c. 200 CE redaction, may not describe 30s CE practice exactly."}
